//! How a searcher seeks, chosen from its automaton.
//!
//! The next place to try is where a match can begin. The more bytes of a
//! match are known before it is found, the fewer places are tried, so the
//! automaton is asked how every match begins, and the first of these
//! that holds is the way:
//!
//! ```text
//! a rule matches no bytes                   every place is tried
//! the rules match one literal of two        the literal is sought, and
//! bytes or more and nothing else            its places are the matches
//! every match begins with the same two      those bytes are sought, and
//! bytes or more                             the automaton matches there
//! the bytes of a later place are rare,       those are sought, and the
//! or far rarer than common first bytes      automaton matches where all
//!                                           the first places fit
//! every byte can begin a match              every place is tried
//! otherwise                                 the next first byte is sought
//! ```
//!
//! One byte that every match begins with is a first byte like any other,
//! and a literal of one byte is found by the finder of its byte. A literal
//! that only a later rule matches is sought as the bytes that every match
//! begins with: the automaton says the rule.
//!
//! No way is chosen by how common a byte is. A finder costs more than
//! reading on to a place that is near, so `seek` tests four bytes one by
//! one before it asks any finder: a byte that the text is full of is met
//! there, and one that the text has rarely is worth the finder, whichever
//! byte it is. The other side of it: where the first of the bytes that
//! every match begins with is within four places of every place, the
//! finder of those bytes is never asked, and every such place is tried
//! with the automaton, as it was without them.
//!
//! The sets of the first places, up to eight, are sought when the one of
//! the least weight, as `sets` weighs a set by the ranks of its bytes, is
//! not the first, and
//!
//! ```text
//! its weight is half that of the first set at most,
//! and less than an eighth of a text,
//! and the first set weighs an eighth of a text or more,
//!     or every byte of the sought set is rare
//! ```
//!
//! Half is on the near side of where seeking a later place was measured
//! to stop paying, in English text and in source code. After a small
//! letter, the four most common letters, which weigh under half of what
//! the letters do, are sought a third to two fifths faster than the
//! first bytes, the six most common a tenth to a sixth faster, the eight
//! most common about as fast, and more of them slower, by up to a tenth,
//! since about as many places are tried as there are first bytes and
//! each costs the test of its sets besides. The eighth of the second
//! line keeps a set that ordinary text is full of from being sought
//! where the first set is so common that half of it still is, as after
//! any byte. There the five most common letters and more, up to all of
//! them, were sought a tenth to two fifths faster than every place is
//! tried, since the test of a set costs less than the automaton does at
//! a place that fails. That is left, so that no set that a text is full
//! of is sought, and a searcher that would skip for such rules skips.
//!
//! The last condition is there because a weight is not a share of the
//! text at hand, and is wrong by several times for single bytes. A first
//! set that weighs an eighth or more is a class of letters or wider: a
//! text is full of it, the finder of its bytes stops at every other
//! place, and any later set of half the weight tries fewer places. Under
//! an eighth the first bytes may well be rare in the text, and their
//! finder then passes it as fast as any: seeking a later set that the
//! text has more of was measured to cost up to nine tenths of the speed,
//! as a point after a digit does in English text. So the later set is
//! sought only when its bytes are rare by their ranks alone, which
//! `sets` says: a `k` after `S` or `s`, and not a colon, a point or a
//! hyphen after a digit, nor an `h` or an `x` after a capital. Those
//! were faster in some texts, a date in source code two and a half
//! times, and slower in others, the same date in English text by half.
//! When the first set is the one of the least weight, its bytes are the
//! first bytes, which are sought as they are: testing the other sets
//! before the automaton is asked was not faster than asking it.
//!
//! A searcher that may build a second automaton, `Searcher::skipping`,
//! skips where it would seek the first bytes or try every place, a match
//! can begin while another is on its way, and the shortest match from a
//! place can have five bytes or more: `skip` says what that does and why
//! the literals are sought as they are. One that seeks the sets goes on
//! seeking them under the same two conditions, with the second automaton
//! for the places that fail.

import core::intrinsic::array;
import std::collections::pure::list::List;
import std::regex::alphabet::bits::Bits;
import std::regex::automaton::Automaton;
import std::regex::automaton::scan;
import std::regex::search::Searcher;
import std::regex::search::Way;

impl Way {
    // How the matches of `automaton` are sought: they can begin with the
    // bytes `first`, and all of them begin with the bytes `prefix`. When
    // the rules match those and nothing else, `cell` is the cell of the
    // state after them, and else 0.
    fn of(automaton: Automaton, first: Bits, prefix: [u8; ?], cell: u32) -> Way {
        if automaton.accepts_empty() || first.len() == 256 { Way::EveryPlace } else {
            if array::dim(prefix, 0) < 2 { Way::FirstBytes } else {
                // The places of a literal are given as matches of the
                // rule 0: when another rule accepts it, the automaton
                // goes on matching, and says which.
                if cell != 0 && scan::rule(automaton, cell) == 0 {
                    Way::Literal
                } else {
                    Way::Prefix
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // The sets of the first places of the matches of `automaton`, eight
    // at most, for a searcher that would seek this way; none when it
    // seeks a literal. The walk for them is left after 1024 states.
    fn first_sets(self: Self, automaton: Automaton) -> List<Bits> {
        match self {
            Literal => List::Nil,
            Prefix => List::Nil,
            _ => automaton.first_sets(8, 1024)
        }
    }

    // The way of a searcher that would seek this way, when the set of
    // the least weight among the first places of a match is that of the
    // place `sought` and weighs `least`, the set of the first place
    // weighs `first`, and `scarce` says whether every byte of the sought
    // set is rare: by the sets when the place is not the first, its
    // weight is half that of the first at most and less than an eighth
    // of a text, which is 8192 parts, and either the first set weighs
    // that eighth or the sought bytes are rare.
    fn or_sets(self: Self, sought: u64, least: u64, first: u64, scarce: bool) -> Way {
        let lighter = sought > 0 && least < 8192 && least * 2 <= first;
        if lighter && (first >= 8192 || scarce) {
            Way::Sets
        } else {
            self
        }
    }

    // Whether a searcher that would seek this way skips instead, or has
    // its places bounded when it seeks the sets, with the automaton of
    // the ends of `automaton`: no literal is sought, a
    // match can begin while another is on its way, and a place that
    // fails can have read four bytes or more, which it has when the
    // shortest match from a place can have five. A place that reads
    // fewer is passed as fast by trying the next one.
    fn gains(self: Self, automaton: Automaton) -> bool {
        match self {
            Literal => false,
            Prefix => false,
            _ => automaton.overlaps() && match automaton.shortest_lengths() {
                Nothing => false,
                Within(_, greatest) => greatest >= 5,
                From(_) => true
            }
        }
    }
}

impl Searcher {
    /// How this searcher comes to the next place to try. Which way is
    /// chosen for which rules is no promise: the matches are the same
    /// whichever it is.
    pub fn way(self: Self) -> Way {
        self.way
    }
}
